Munich 1856 : Four maps changing your view of the city
A mistaken institution where young people are expected to meet today .
A chapel where the underground tunnel is digged . The church was built in the 18th century .
Small @-@ scale gardeners manage the former farmland .
The oldest official map of Munich promotes exciting stories to the world .
It is annoying when maps are not up @-@ to @-@ date .
Everyone who had to get upset knows this , because the auto @-@ navy showed a green meadow instead of a bypass .
The historic maps of the Bayern @-@ Atlas digital , an offer of the Bavarian geo @-@ resort to the state government , are anything but up @-@ to @-@ date - but that is why they are particularly revealing .
Especially when compared to the latest online maps .
Then it becomes clear how cities and municipalities in the spread area of the Munich Merkur district have changed since the 19th century .
The digital Bayern @-@ Atlas is a patchwork pile that you can &apos;t recognize right away .
The large overview map has included more detailed maps for cities and municipalities , so @-@ called original sheets .
According to the Bavarian survey management , these were established between 1810 and 1864 and originated from the first survey conducted in Bavaria .
Your client : Napoleon .
The French army leader occupied Bavaria since the spring of 1800 during the Second Coalition War with Austria .
Napoleon wanted to have an overview of the occupied country and commissioned a topographic acceptance of Bavaria - for military purposes of course .
Even though the French troops finally left with the peace of Lunéville on February 9 , 1801 , they were the present neighbours who created the idea of a Bavarian map .
Elector Max IV Joseph continued to drive the project forward , founded the Topographic Bureau - and thus the Bavarian Survey Management .
The original journal for Munich was published in 1856 and , like all other publications , is available on the Bavarian State Library &apos;s website .
The comparison of the old and the present is most impressive with the example of the state capital .
The city has expanded in all directions and has grown together .
An example of this is Lower Sendling , which still appears relatively isolated in the 1856 map .
Sendling was incorporated as a district of Munich 21 years after the map was drawn up , in 1877 .
Today Lower Sendling is located in the heart of the city .
The houses Untersendlings are concentrated around 1856 along the current Plingan Road , which was named after Georg Sebastian Plinganser in 1878 , a leader of the Bavarian peasant revolt against Austria in 1705 .
Where the Westpark is today , nothing but fields are seen - at first glance : at the zoom in , a smaller map appears with dozens of lines and numbers : handwritten pieces of land from the so @-@ called &quot; initial record , &quot; from which the property tax was derived .
Small parcels no longer occupy farmers , but small gardeners of the &quot; Land in Sun &quot; plant .
A large green area is located north of Lower Sendling .
The Terezín , the site of the Oktoberfest since 1810 , was in fact still a meadow in the 19th century - and not a concrete desert .
On the other side of the Isar , the historical map gives an insight into the history of psychiatric medicine .
In the original newspaper , the street name &quot; am Gasteig &quot; or &quot; Auf der Lüften &quot; is not only read in the area of the present @-@ day Au @-@ Haidhausen district , but also in large letters &quot; Irren Anstalt . &quot;
Yes , here on Auerfeldstraße people were once locked in with madness .
In 1858 / 59 the Munich Circle Irrenanstalt &quot; Auf der Auer Lüften &quot; was founded .
In 1872 , Dr. Bernhard de Gudden led the institution - the psychiatrist associated with the death of King Louis II .
&quot; He was progressive for his time and ended , for example , the chain of so @-@ called irritants .
He also prohibited the staff from using force against the occupants , &quot; says Hermann Wilhelm , director of the Haidhausen Museum , who has been working with the Anstalt for an exhibition .
280 patients had been accommodated in the first place , and 500 seats were extended later .
At one point this capacity was not sufficient - the reason for the construction of the Kreisirrenanstalt in Haar in 1905 .
The Isar Amper Clinic Munich @-@ Ost , one of the largest psychiatrists in Germany , has been located there since 2006 .
Young people are now meeting up in the former Auer Anstalt area in the Salesianum , Munich .
The order acquired the building complex in 1920 and transformed it into a youth home with training companies .
To the west lies the football pitch of the TSV München Ost , east residential houses from the 1980s .
In 2015 the Casa Don Bosco Children &apos;s House was built , in architectural style , on the basis of the Irrenanstalt &apos;s original structure , which was destroyed during bombing during the Second World War .
The Marienplatz was listed as &quot; Main Square &quot; around 1856 , and the Kaufingerstraße was called &quot; Kaufinger Gasse . &quot;
The Second World War brought a decisive change in the heart of Munich .
The Allied bombs in December 1944 pulled many holes into the city centre .
One large one remained until today - at the Marienhof .
The English Institute , small business houses and a medieval pit chapel once stood at Marienhof , where the preparatory work is done and the soil is cut for the second S @-@ Bahn route on 5 April 2017 .
The slider allows you to compare the old Munich map with the aerial picture of today - and fill the hole at the Marienhof .
Civil Rights Group publishes Travel Warning for Missouri
The National Association for the Promotion of Colorful People issued a warning to colorful people travelling to Missouri on the basis of the discriminating policies and racial attacks of the state .
&quot; The NAACP Travel Advisory for Missouri , which is to be in force until August 28 , 2017 , calls on African @-@ American travelers , visitors and Missouri to pay particular attention to trips by the State and to exercise extreme caution , as a number of questionable , race @-@ based incidents occur and are recorded throughout the state lately , &quot; the group &apos;s statement states .
A recent Missouri law making it harder for people to make discrimination claims and the prosecution authorities of the state that disproportionately target minorities , prompted the group to issue the Travel Warning , said NAACP .
&quot; They have violated civil rights , people pass through .
They are stopped because of their skin colour , they are beaten up or killed , &quot; said the president of Missouri NAACP , Rod Chapel , Kansas City Star .
&quot; We hear complaints in such a frequency that we have not heard before . &quot;
It is the first such warning issued by the organization to a state in the USA .
The group quoted incidents such as racist crimes against black students at the University of Missouri and the death of Tory Sanders , 28 , a black man from Tennessee .
Sanders died in questionable circumstances early this year , having lost petrol during his trip through the state and having been taken into custody by Missouri &apos;s police without being accused of any crime .
The Council also points to a recent report by the Missouri Attorney General &apos;s Office , which shows that black drivers are stopped 75 % more frequently than white drivers in the state .
&quot; The recommendation is that people are aware and inform their families , friends and employees about what could happen in Missouri , &quot; Chapel said .
&quot; People must be prepared whether they bring in security money or let their relatives know that they are travelling through the state . &quot;
Missouri recorded 100 hate crimes in 2015 , according to the latest figures from the FBI &apos;s hate crime reporting programme , which put the state in 16th position in terms of the number of such injuries .
The Travel Warning is also a response to a new Missouri law that would make it harder to sue a company for discrimination in housing or employment .
Previously , the American Civil Liberties Union ( ACLU ) had issued travel warnings for Texas and Arizona after the immigration laws passed by the states compelling local law enforcement agencies to arrest people for immigrant violations , which ACLU believes will further raise the racial profile .
Travel warnings are usually issued by the Foreign Office for other countries , but interest groups have recently used the measure to respond to certain laws and trends within the USA .
Weidezaunprojekt is elementary
