Block critics on Facebook : Do not apply for a public office if you cannot stand the heating of voters .
The father of our country knew something of bad press .
Americans loved George Washington , but it was not long before the newspapers beat him for all of the reasons , from domestic politics to his political principles .
He cried , certainly , in the critic .
But he did not silence his critics .
For in 1783 , Washington said : &quot; Freedom of speech can be removed - and , silly and dumb , we are led to slaughter like sheep . &quot;
This brings me to Maryland to Governor Larry Hogan , who has to work to be more like Washington .
Hogans employees have blocked and deleted the contributions of at least 450 people who expressed their opinion on their official Facebook page .
And the American Civil Liberties Union sued him for this at the beginning of this week .
The bar of the governor dismissed the lawsuit as frivol , and the online comment was full of a counter @-@ reaction with &quot; Well , and ? . &quot;
&quot; It &apos;s just Facebook , &quot; many people said .
But it is important .
And it is especially important when it is a type like Hogan .
This is a republican governor in an overwhelming democratic state , which is amazingly popular .
It has the second highest approval of the 50 governors of the nation .
Hogan is not a reactionary heat head .
He has shown a firm hand in the management of his state and strict adherence to the principles .
He was also very clever in using Facebook as his primary means of communicating with his electorate and playing his hairy head after chemotherapy on his side .
Thus , blocking people who come to the side of the governor - a public forum , which is known as official and paid by employees with public dollars - is unnecessary and ultimately dangerous .
In an interview with The Washington Post , Hogan &apos;s spokeswoman Amelia Chasse defended the governor &apos;s actions and argued that blocking the commentaries was nothing but moderation .
But it is too easy to use the image of Trolls or Spammers or hate @-@ filled people who are swinging the whip online .
The post spoke with some of the real people who were blocked by Hogan .
And these are only true people who speak with their elected leaders : a teacher , an entrepreneur and a pastor , not a troll .
They all said that their comments were respectful , thoughtful and not proactive .
The priest quoted the Bible in his post and appealed to Hogan &apos;s Catholic faith .
Lawyer Lakshmi Sarma Ramani of Bowie , Md . , was not hateful , but she asked about hate crimes .
&quot; I politely commented that I was disappointed that there was no reaction to hate crimes and other news , &quot; she wrote in the comment section of the news program &quot; The Post . &quot;
&quot; I don &apos;t like this idea either , when a number of people take a stand on the same subject , some disregard it as a so @-@ called collective effort and do not recognize it as a large group of citizens concerned . &quot;
What the governor &apos;s staff called a &quot; concentrated spam attack &quot; would probably be called &quot; intercession . &quot;
The Facebook era makes it easy to cut a message by simply blocking a critic or deleting a negative comment .
It is much cleaner than in the past if the same thing had been done , that people were sent to collect and burn newspapers with critical editorials or to arrest and silence demonstrators .
But that is exactly what is happening digitally .
Hogan is not the first public official to be criticized for having reproached Facebook and other social media .
President Trump is sued by Twitter users who have been blocked by his Twitter feed .
One of the first milestones in this question fell last week in Virginia .
The chairman of the board of supervisors of Loudoun County violated the First Amendment , according to US District Judge James C. Cacheris in Alexandria , when he banished a voter from his Facebook page .
And in Kentucky Governor Matt Bevin ( R ) also received a visit from ACLU for his use of Facebook and Twitter .
This should not be so heavy .
At the time of Washington , the affordable port had a similar effect to the Internet .
The number of newspapers multiplied between 1776 and 1800 , and anonymous writers swallowed in his leadership .
Even then , Washington had anonymous trolls .
The people who used pseudonyms like &quot; Juricola , &quot; &quot; Valerius , &quot; &quot; Belisarius &quot; and &quot; Portius &quot; wrote all the letters to newspapers that suppressed Washington &apos;s decisions .
Petitions that criticized his position on the Amity Treaty , trade and shipping with the UK overwhelmed his office , according to the historical documents collected by the George Washington Project &apos;s online papers .
But he did not silence them .
Freedom of speech , disagreement and discourses are the basis of our nation .
And true guidance means accepting it .
