Poverty and death in Indonesia &apos;s Goldland
When Bardina Degei cooks dinner , she does not use a stove .
It rarely uses a pot .
In her wooden house in Enarotali , capital of the Paniai ruler in the unsettled Indonesian province of Papua , the housewife usually puts a sweet potato - locally called &quot; nota &quot; - directly into the fireplace .
After half an hour , the chopped tubes are taken and swallowed up with eager , unwashed hands .
Degei sits on the mud floor - she has no furniture - where she also carries out her daily work , for example washing clothes with bad water from the nearby swamp .
A bucket in a roofless room serves as a latrine .
The youngest of her husband &apos;s four wives was not assigned any fields .
Polygamy is the norm .
Of course , late working can be dangerous : most villagers are unemployed , and many drink heavy , and there are soldiers here .
&quot; Nobody dares to walk around the village after 5 p.m. , &quot; she says .
It is a rare insight into the daily life of the highlands of Papua , a former Dutch colony , which was admitted in 1969 after a controversial referendum in Indonesia , when only 1,026 elders were asked to vote by a public hiding in front of the occupying forces .
An existing movement that was stir up for independence against the Dutch rule quickly turned against the Jakarta government , which controls the region and excludes foreign journalists or law observers from it .
In 2003 the province was officially divided into Papua and West Papua , with the independent part of Papua New Guinea covering the eastern part of the island .
Enarotali is as remote as it is , including a 90 @-@ minute flight from the provincial capital Jayapura to Nabire and a 5 @-@ hour morning car ride with the car .
There is no public transportation .
The city of about 19,000 inhabitants consists of wooden houses surrounded by bamboo fences and corrugated roofs , converted into different yellow @-@ brown tones by rust .
Very few Indonesians have made their journey here , let alone journalists and virtually no foreigners .
Before Christian missionaries arrived , Mee Pago Papuans reverted to a god called Uga Tamee .
There were other changes as well .
&quot; We were not used to wearing these clothes , &quot; says Degei , pointing out her lively , hand @-@ woven turban , her dark shirt and her bright skirt .
&quot; In the past we had only leaves on our bodies . &quot;
Papua is Indonesia &apos;s poorest province , where 28 % of the population live below the poverty line and have some of the worst infant mortality and literacy rates in Asia .
But it is also Indonesia &apos;s gold country .
Grasberg , the largest and most profitable gold mine in the world , owned by the Freeport McMoran of Phoenix , is just 60 miles away from Paniai , a high @-@ land province of New Jersey with 153,000 inhabitants .
In 2015 alone , Freeport mined gold and copper to the value of $ 3.1 billion .
In addition , Papua has wood resources valued at an estimated $ 78 billion .
These riches are a source of misery for the Papua , which ensures that Indonesia &apos;s powerful military remains a stifling presence .
A 2005 survey in the New York Times reported that Freeport paid local military personnel and units almost $ 20 million between 1998 and 2004 , up to $ 150,000 to a single officer .
Papuan demands for greater autonomy threaten these golden goose and are treated mercilessly .
According to activists , more than 500,000 Papua were killed and thousands were raped , tortured and imprisoned by the Indonesian military since 1969 .
Massacre killings in Papua &apos;s tribal highlands in the 1970 &quot; s became genocide , according to the Asian Human Rights Commission .
In the region alone , the Indonesian police arrested more than 3.900 peaceful demonstrators last year .
&quot; We Will All Loss &quot; is the report of the archdiocese of Brisbane in 2016 , which contains reports of the atrocities of the previous year , such as unlawful executions and torture - rape and electric strikes are especially popular , according to another report - and the brutal crackdown of peaceful demonstrations .
&quot; It is difficult to count the number of victims , as every week incidents happen , &quot; says Andreas Harsono , Indonesian researcher for Human Rights Watch .
The screws have become stronger as Papuas resources are bringing an influx of settlers from other parts of Indonesia .
The population of 3.5 million inhabitants of the province is 83 % Christian , but the demographic trend is changing as Muslim economic migrants come from the populous islands Java , Borneo , Sumatra and Sulawesi .
Javanese Warung Cantines sell fried chicken and Gado @-@ Gado , a mixed vegetables with peanut sauce .
Local people are fighting to keep up with the game .
&quot; Migrants have begun to sell chicken and vegetables on the traditional market cheaper than local Papua , &quot; explains Abeth You , a 24 @-@ year @-@ old Paniai native who has moved to work in the provincial capital Jayapura .
&quot; Papua , the Mum Mum &#91; the women &#93; of Papua , tasted their market . &quot;
President Joko Widodo of Indonesia , known as Jokowi , promised during his election campaign in 2014 to tackle the inequalities and the violations of the law in Papua .
The former carpenter secured 27 of the 29 districts of Papua - including Paniai - on the way to the presidential palace in Jakarta .
But in Papua little has changed and today the locals feel betrayed .
&quot; Our hearts have been broken because we voted in 2014 for Jokowi , with the expectation that he will fulfill our hopes that justice will be restored , &quot; You said .
In fact , just two months after Jokowis &apos;s inauguration , Paniai suffered a low point in October .
On December 7 , 2014 , a group of 11 children were out and sang Christmas songs in front of a campfire in Enarotali , when two Indonesian soldiers on a motorcycle broke through the darkness .
The shocked children told them to turn on their headlights .
One of the soldiers took umbrage on their clay and later returned with four soldiers , said local Pastor Yavedt Tebai .
The soldiers who had drunk , hunted and beat the group with their rifle , said victims and witnesses .
Then one of the soldiers fired into the group of children .
A child , the 16 @-@ year @-@ old Yulianus Yeimo , was so badly beaten that he fell into coma .
A few hours later , the building of the nearby government commission was set on fire and things escalated the following day .
About 1,000 young Papua men , women and children gathered in a football pitch in front of the local police station and the military commander centre to demand justice .
They wore ceremonial hunting arches and performed the Waita dance - constantly in the circle and imitating a bird &apos;s song - of Papuas Mee Pago tribe .
Some demonstrators started to throw stones at police and military posts .
When the mood was heated , the soldier was sent a command via an internal radio : &quot; When the masses resist for more than three times , they shoot them dead , &quot; says an official TIME document , which was not released to the local media .
Yeremias Kayame , 56 , the head of the Kego Koto quarter of Enarotali , saw the imminent danger and called for peace and quiet and urged the crowd to return home .
Nobody was in the mood to listen .
&quot; When I turned around , I was suddenly shot into my left wrist , &quot; he said to TIME on the veranda of his colorfully painted wooden house .
Kayame still does not know who fired , but says that the ball came from the ranks of the soldiers gathered here .
&quot; It was crowded , many shots were fired , &quot; he adds .
The local man Alfius Youw was hit three times , so his cousin , who witnessed the shooting .
&quot; I ran to him and investigated his body to make sure that he was , &quot; Yohanes , who like many Indonesians only bears one name , said the TIME in a gloomy mood .
&quot; I saw that he was dead ... I kissed him . &quot;
The Papua police chief inspector , Yotje Mende , told reporters that his officers wanted to &quot; secure &quot; their station because they were attacked .
&quot; We must defend ourselves when people threaten to kill us , &quot; said the Papua police spokesman , Commissioner Pudjo Sulistiyo , in 2015 .
&quot; It is about life and death . &quot;
According to Human Rights Watch , five young demonstrators were killed and many more were injured .
The news about the killings came to Jakarta the following day &apos; filtered &apos; .
Three weeks ago , Jokowi made a passionate speech in Jayapura , expressing his sympathy for the families of the victims and vowing to tackle the historical abuses in Papua .
&quot; I want this case to be resolved immediately , so that it will not happen any more in the future , &quot; he said .
In October 2016 , Minister of Security Wiranto said that he set up an extrajudicial mechanism for the settlement of historical violations of human rights .
But the excuses started almost immediately .
&quot; Most injuries occurred a long time ago .
Some were in the 90 &quot; s and early 2000 &quot; s .
The point is that we are committed to tackle these violations , but there are processes that need to be completed , &quot; he said .
Then , on June 5 , in Jakarta , he withdrew from the discussion with TIME and said that he had no plans to set up a complaint mechanism in Papua .
Instead , &quot; everything is regulated by law , &quot; he said .
In the wake of the recent war in East Timor , the United Nations has declared that 11 cases of human rights violations have been settled in Papua , including the panai incident , and that the country has been accused of &quot; crimes against humanity &quot; which claimed more than 1,000 deaths in East Timor &apos;s bloody independence vote of 1999 .
Families of the panic victims received such claims with fierce infidelity .
&quot; I have been interviewed four times in the last three years , but there has been no progress , &quot; Yohanes says .
&quot; I &apos;m tired . &quot;
Years later , he says that he is still living in fear .
&quot; I &apos;m afraid , &quot; he says .
&quot; I am afraid of being arrested by the military , I &apos;m afraid to be shot . &quot;
His brother Yacobus repeated the view that the people in Paniai are afraid to discuss the incident .
He says that he was defeated by the military after helping to bury four of the victims .
&quot; After I had buried the corpses , the military searched for me , &quot; he says .
The shootings have not stopped .
On Tuesday , the Indonesian police hit villagers in Paniais neighbourhood Deiyai .
During a confrontation between villagers and the manager of a construction company that refused to bring a man to hospital , one person died and 17 others were injured , including children .
The man , Ravianus Douw , 24 @-@ year @-@ old , who was drowned when he was fishing in a nearby river , died on his way to hospital .
The excited villagers protested in front of the company &apos;s location office .
The police said that villagers threw stones at officers who responded by firing warning shots .
But locals say that the mobile brigade ( Indonesian paramilitary police ) began to shoot at the crowd and killed one .
&quot; We were so in panic , we are afraid that there will be revenge , &quot; says Dominggu Badii , 29 , who lives near the hospital and witnessed how the injured were quickly delivered here , says the TIME .
&quot; I have hidden in my house for two days . &quot;
The Deiyai Parliament has called for the officers to be brought to account and the mobile police brigade to be withdrawn from the area .
Paniai has always been a source of trouble for the Indonesian government .
The lack of meaningful development strengthens the dissatisfaction of the tribal members Mee , Moni , Dani and Damal who live in the green highland of Papua .
Many joined the &quot; Free Papua Movement &quot; ( OPM ) , the rebel army , which claims to defend the rights of Papua by initiating sporadic attacks and kidnappings of Indonesian soldiers .
Some of the top OPM leaders are from Paniai , including Tadius Yogi and Daniel Yudas Kogoya .
In response , thousands of people have been arrested in panic over the past few years and arbitrarily taken into custody under the excuse of &quot; safeguarding national sovereignty . &quot;
Some never return .
Among the people of Papua , Paniai is known as &quot; a tragic , forgotten place . &quot;
Poverty strengthens dissatisfaction .
The little rice that is sold in Enarotali is too expensive for the locals .
Bread is just as out of reach .
People here grow everything they eat , especially nota and fruit and leafy vegetables .
The agriculture is the task of women who are able to work on four or five fields of sweet potato .
They keep most of the harvest for the family , while the rest is sold on the local market .
Ten pieces by Nota cost just 10,000 Indonesian rupees ( 75 cents ) .
Over the years , economic inequalities between Papua residents and new migrants have grown , who have arrived in larger numbers since the opening of new flight routes to Nabire airport .
The few jobs that exist in the region are usually directed to the better @-@ educated and wealthier migrants .
Papua rarely has the capital or skills to run its own businesses in a competitive way .
&quot; Young people are not interested in staying in the village ... because there are no jobs or money here , &quot; says John Gobai , chairman of the Paniai tribal council .
Isolation keeps the eyes of the world away from Papua .
In addition , restrictions on reporting by international media remain tense .
At the beginning of the year , French journalists Franck Escudie and Basille Longchamp were deported from Papua for lack of coordination with related institutions , although they were granted rare permission to film .
According to Phelim Kine , Deputy Director of Human Rights Watch in Asia , the promises of Jokowis &apos;s campaign to lift the restrictions on reporting in order to promote transparency and development have not been realised .
&quot; There are new threats for foreign journalists trying to report from Indonesia &apos;s most troubled eastern provinces of Papua and West Papua : visa refusal and a black list , &quot; he said in a statement .
A lack of press control means that international pressure on the Indonesian government remains largely limited to the Papua &apos;s immediate neighbors .
In March six Pacific states - Tonga , Nauru , Palau , Tuvalu , the Marshall Islands and the Solomon Islands - asked the UN Human Rights Council to investigate the &quot; various and widespread injuries &quot; in Papua including the panic shooting .
The same countries have historically supported OPM .
The speaker of Indonesia &apos;s Ministry of Foreign Affairs Arrmanatha Nasir rejected the group &apos;s accusations and told journalists in Jakarta : &quot; Indonesia still has a democratic system and there are free media , making it difficult to conceal the evidence of human rights cases . &quot;
The local people want more foreign governments to take this into account .
When an official delegation from the Netherlands , led by the human rights ambassador Kees Van Baar , visited Jayapura on 4 May , locals broke their silence and afflicted him &quot; We want freedom , &quot; according to a source that also participated in the meeting but wants to remain anonymous .
Indonesia has another presidential election in 2019 , but the Papua say that they are unlikely to vote again for Jokowi .
&quot; Jokowi is a man with good intentions , but he is surrounded by the people involved in the Paniai shots , &quot; says Gobai , chairman of the tribal council .
He wants Jokowi to know that the Paniai people , apart from being under the permanent threat of a predatory military , are in poor educational and health services .
Gobai says that the panic people , like other Papua , consider their vote for Jokowi as a &quot; fault &quot; which he must repay .
&quot; They don &apos;t need money , they just want justice , &quot; he says .
Despite the threats and intimidations , the families of the Paniai Shooter Shooter Shoot Victims carried out a final symbolic act of the Trotskyi : the body of a victim was buried on a site just opposite the police and the military station .
They know that they may never receive justice , but at least they don &apos;t let the people responsible forget their crimes .
&quot; A member of our family was killed , &quot; Yacobus said with a smudged head .
&quot; What else can we do ? &quot;
Fire captures Dubai &apos;s Torch skyscrapers for the second time .
A fire in Dubai on Friday found its way through one of the world &apos;s highest residential towers , forcing hundreds of residents to flee the 337 @-@ metre @-@ high 79 @-@ floor building .
It is the second fire that sweeps through the tower in just two years , which is coincidentally called the Torch Tower ( torch tower ) .
