This is because the company cannot prevent dissatisfied customers from walking away in droves .
EPO Chief Executive Pruitt rejects the reduction of emissions due to the delay in the Obama @-@ Era rules .
One day after being sued by 15 countries , Scott Pruitt , the Environmental Protection Agency &apos;s chief , retracted his earlier decision to delay the implementation of the Obama era rules to reduce the emissions of smog @-@ causing air pollutants .
Pruitt presented the change when his agency proved to be more responsive to the needs of the state &apos;s environmental authorities than previous administrations .
He did not mention the lawsuit brought against his previous position in a federal court of appeal .
The deadline for countries to meet the 2015 standards for ground @-@ level ozone was 1 October .
Pruitt announced in June that he would delay compliance for one year , giving his authority more time to study the plan and to avoid &quot; interfering in local decisions or hampering economic growth . &quot;
Pruitt , who was prosecutor of Oklahoma before his appointment by President Donald Trump , was for a long time a reliable opponent of stricter environmental protection regulations .
Since his arrival in Washington , Pruitt has repeatedly switched to blocking or delaying regulations which the chemical industry and the fossil fuel industry are opposed to .
The sudden reversal of Wednesday is the latest legal setback for the rucksack &apos;s regulatory rollback agenda .
Last month a federal court in Washington ruled that the EPO manager had exceeded his authority by trying to delay the implementation of an Obama Administration rule that obliged oil and gas companies to monitor and reduce methane leaks .
In an opinion published on Wednesday evening , Pruitt suggested that his turnaround in ozone standards would simply reinforce the EPO &apos;s readiness to cooperate with the states through the complex process of timely compliance with the new standards .
&quot; Among former administrations , EPO has often failed to meet the deadlines for implementation and has then waited to be accused by groups of activists and others , and agreed on the deadline for implementation , &quot; said Pruitt , who has accused EPO of more than a dozen cases in his previous job .
&quot; We do not believe in legal regulation and we take deadlines seriously .
We also take seriously the law and the authority that it gives us . &quot;
Nevertheless , the EPO opinion stated that Pruitt will at some point use its &quot; authority to delay and all other legally available powers &quot; to ensure that the rules are &quot; based on a solid policy and the best available information . &quot;
The Republicans in Congress are pressing for a more comprehensive revision of the ozone rules .
A bill approved last month is intended to delay the implementation of the 2015 rules for at least eight years .
The measure has not yet been put to the vote in the Senate .
Eric Schneiderman , New York Attorney General , who headed the coalition of the states that the EPO had sued this week , said that the group intends to maintain the legal pressure .
&quot; The EPO &apos;s reversal - after our lawsuits - is an important health and safety benefit for these 6.7 million New Yorkers and the more than 115 million Americans directly affected by the smog that flows into their communities , &quot; Schneiderman said .
New York joined California , Connecticut , Delaware , Illinois , Iowa , Maine , Massachusetts , Minnesota , New Mexico , Oregon , Pennsylvania , Rhode Island , Vermont and Washington , and the District of Columbia .
Ground @-@ ozone is formed when harmful substances from cars , power plants , oil refineries , chemical plants and other sources in the atmosphere respond to sunlight .
The resulting smog can cause serious respiratory problems in sensitive groups of people and can lead to thousands of premature deaths every year .
Representatives of the public health system and environmentalists cheered about Pruitt &apos;s surprising change of course .
&quot; It is worrying how much pressure it takes to get this reasonable step from the person responsible for the protection of the air we breathe , &quot; Lori Ann Burd from the Center for Biological Diversity said .
&quot; We must continue to fight against the ideological crusade of the Trump government to turn its back on polluters and their special interests . &quot;
Tumlingen celebrates 750 years of St. Hilarius Church
750 years of St. Hilarius church : On the occasion of the anniversary of the mountain church , the old cemetery in Tumlingen is invited to a serenade on Sunday , July 16 , at 4.30 p.m .
Whether its idyllic location and beautiful views of the village , the historic St. Hilarius complex is a popular place to linger .
Visitors to the sixth Benefiz Serenade can be enchanted by this powerful place .
The friends and sponsors of Tumlingens have made the rescue and maintenance of the oldest building in the village their concern .
Walter Martini emphasizes : &quot; It is a unique village gem . &quot;
In 1267 , the St. Hilarius Chapel was first mentioned in a document in the German Document Book : Count Hermann of Sulz sold &quot; the mountain with the parish church of Tumlingen and the Vogtei &quot; to Magister Eberhard of Horb and his brother Dietrich for 20 Mark Silver .
According to the document , the Tumlinger Mountain , apart from the former parish church , also included a splendid court , to which the church or the patron &apos;s right belonged and not the other way round .
So people lived there as well .
As the description of the Upper Austrian Freudenstadt from 1858 shows , the third , the noble village , was next to the suburbs and the Oberdorf , which are still visible today in the village of Tumlingen .
Old maps dating back to the 1600 &apos;s , support this historical background .
The right to patron was derived from the law of the early medieval times .
A lord who built , equipped and occupied a church with a parish priest , remained the owner of the church and could also inherit this church .
The right to appoint a pastor or or to propose a new pastor to the bishop was connected with this .
Three altars were in the church and two bells on the tower .
The larger of the two was torn at Christmas 1673 .
The newly purchased bell sounded in 1692 , but was later abandoned in 1942 during the Second World War .
In the 30 @-@ year war ( 1616 to 1648 ) the windows were shinged and the bell parts stolen .
The roof was leaking .
In 1796 , French troops were detained near the church .
They broke out the pulpit and church chairs and used them as firewood .
The remains of the church were in danger of falling down in 1809 .
Until 1869 , the Tumlinger Hilarius cemetery served as the last resting place for the dead from Hörschweiler .
In 1823 the cemetery was expanded and the Hilarius church was discontinued .
Only the church tower was left standing .
In the New Year &apos;s Eve 1834 a storm split the church tower and half of it collapsed .
In order to prevent misfortune , the tower was shut down .
Only the tower dwarf , which still exists today , remained as a witness to the 750 @-@ year @-@ old church history .
Today Horst Kallenberger and Rolf @-@ Dieter Wasem maintain the Hilarius plant as a successor to Dieter Dockhorn by the &quot; Schaffigen Renters . &quot;
The Friends and Patrons of Tumlingens invite to the old cemetery for the 6th Serenade on Sunday , July 16 , to the St. Hilarius Chapel .
The composer is the composer of the Kepler @-@ Gymnasium Freudenstadt and with speeches by Kurt Kübler and Reinhold Matteis .
The local leader , Hartmut Romann , will speak the final word .
The programme begins at 4.30 p.m .
Admittance is available from 3.30 p.m .
To finish the serenade , a nightcap will be held in front of the cemetery .
&quot; Enjoy a summer evening under the shade of old trees and with a wide view over meadows towards the forest , &quot; the organizers highlight the fantastic view .
The serenade is only held in dry weather .
The organisers hope for many supporters to preserve the historic St. Hilarius plant , which is important for tumlingen and listeners .
Friends and sponsors of tumlingens feel particularly committed to the care of their homeland , as Serenade organizer Walter Martini confirms .
Association wants approval of windbikes to be verified
&quot; Boisheim fights back &quot; does not see any legal requirement for the construction and has therefore appealed to the Landrat .
The Boisheim Association has asked Landrat Andreas Coenen ( CDU ) to withdraw his permission for the construction of wind power plants in the Boisheimer Nette .
