But when a nozzle was thrown around the neck of Roger , almost everyone in the public was really sighting .
Since 2005 , civil rights activists have returned to Moore &apos;s Ford Bridge to imitate the night in which two black couples - Roger and Dorothy Malcom , George and Mae Murray Dorsey - were lumped by Ku Klux Klan in 1946 .
No one has ever been accused of the murders .
&quot; It is really incomprehensible that not one person has been arrested in these years - although we see them in our communities , although we know who they are , &quot; Tyrone Brooks said , who is helping to organise the post @-@ production .
&quot; It &apos;s a dirt spot on a story , but it &apos;s a burden for our souls . &quot;
Brooks is a 71 @-@ year @-@ old former congressman of the state of Georgia and a civil rights activist for a lifetime .
For him , the replication serves as a dramatic call for action and represents an annual reminder to the Monroe community that an injustice was never corrected .
&quot; We want prosecution , we want to end , we want recovery , we want reconciliation , but first we need to get justice , &quot; he said .
&quot; We cannot achieve recovery and reconciliation until we receive justice . &quot;
A forensic doctor lays a bed cloth over the corpse of one of the victims of the Moore @-@ Ford Lynchen on 27 July 1946 .
According to FBI files received from NBC News , more than 50 men from Monroe and the surrounding counties were suspected in the Lynch Process .
But none has ever been accused .
Although the suspects have been kept at large for decades , civil rights activists say that the double motivation for the Lynch actions was always known throughout the city : politics and sex .
In April 1946 , a decision of the Supreme Court enabled the black citizens of Georgia to vote for the first time in the summer pre @-@ election campaign .
About the same time as the election , black color guard Roger Malcom burst a white landowner , Barnett Hester , during a fight - allegedly for a woman .
Brooks said that the rumor had been that Hester had slept with Malcoms Mrs. Dorothy and that the baby she was wearing was not her husband &apos;s .
On 25 July 1946 , Loy Harrison , a prominent white landowner , paid $ 600 to release Malcom from prison , according to the FBI .
Dorothy and her cousins Mae Murray and George Dorsey accompanied him to prison .
But when they moved from prison and approached the Moore &apos;s Ford Bridge , a Lynchmob surrounded the car .
&quot; As it turns out , it was Harrison who prepared and arranged the murders , &quot; concludes an FBI report .
&quot; It is also obvious that there was a plot between the state and local prosecutions , which not only participated in the murders , removed the evidence and covered up the identity of witnesses . &quot;
Brooks formulates it even more succinctly .
&quot; It was a right @-@ to @-@ vote massacre , &quot; he said .
&quot; They were all killed to send a message to black people in this community : &quot; When you register and when you vote , you will be met here . &quot;
For almost a decade Cassandra Greene has been managing the annual Moore Ford Bridge Lynch @-@ Morde edition .
Their depiction is vivid , moving and is based on a kind of deep racism that many would like to believe does not exist in America anymore .
It &apos;s hard to look , and even harder to look away , but Greene said that &apos;s the point .
&quot; We don &apos;t want to talk about lynching , because we are then confronted with its ugliness in all of us , &quot; she said .
&quot; But I cannot establish a relationship with you if you don &apos;t want to listen to me or not want to hear what I have gone through . &quot;
The rehearsals for this year &apos;s production began in June , and on the first day almost 30 actors - a dozen white and the rest black - joined a room listening to Greene start a well @-@ established overview of the piece .
Wade Marbaugh is Greene &apos;s co @-@ director here .
He has played the role of boss clan member for years , but he said the role will never be easier .
&quot; I feel dirty because I &apos;m not so , &quot; Marbaugh said .
&quot; But I think it is very important to keep this story alive , because we don &apos;t want to return to those times . &quot;
On the other side of the room , the actors Darrius Bradshaw and Nick Rascona , who play Roger Malcom and Barnett Hester , tried their lines .
The piece begins with Malcom , the hester who cries and afflicts him , furious that he slept with his wife Dorothy .
&quot; I said n --- he , disappearing of my property ! &quot;
Rascona repels the fire .
There is a break .
And then everyone ( at least all black actors ) breaks out in laughter .
Greene laughs the loudest .
&quot; What ? ! , &quot; Rascona asked .
He is alarmed and confused .
Was I too hard ?
&quot; No , &quot; Greene said , still kicking .
&quot; I don &apos;t think that white people said &apos; n --- he &apos; .
&quot; But , &quot; one of the other black actors quipped .
&quot; You only have to hit the hard &apos; R. &apos; .
And it is exactly there that the energy shifts in the room , a sense of comradeship replaces the unease .
Everyone is busy getting it done right , not only because of the game , but also because of the memory of the four people who were killed .
The scene starts again .
This time , when Rascona says the N word , he says it sharply and forcefully .
Absolutely nobody laughs .
This repetition ends violently and quickly .
There are cries , shots and then silence .
A woman dressed in a mourning dress stands above the corpse of the actors and performs Billie Holidays &quot; mourning praises &quot; Strange Fruit . &quot;
Your voice breaks with every second word .
About a hundred metres behind it , a group of about four dozen spectators sweat and tears vanish from the eyes .
The audience is divided almost evenly between black and white .
A couple has traveled from Ithaca , New York , to see the performance , many just drop down the street .
They have experienced Georgias &apos;s crushing moisture every almost eight hours when Brooks takes them on a pilgrimage to the tombs of Malcoms and Dorseys .
Until this point the murders remained an anecdote , but now , when you are on the same land where the Lynch murders took place , the air feels ghostly .
When the last notes of the song end , Greene invites the crowd to come closer and inspect the body name .
Children scourge at their heads , register the fake blood and the silent body and whisper towards each other .
&quot; They are not really dead , &quot; a little boy murmurs as if he wanted to remember them .
After a few minutes Greene thanked the crowd - and suddenly it is over .
The actors throw the Lynchseil away and wipe out their tears .
Bradshaw embraces Marbaugh , the man in the suit that the clan member played .
Nick Rascona embraces two of his fellow players and then crushes in their arms , the emotional burden of the performance finally breaks through with him .
&quot; It &apos;s okay , &quot; whispers one of the women .
&quot; We know that this is not in your heart .
You did it for one reason . &quot;
Later , the audience and the actors sit down for dinner .
A white couple from Atlanta chat at a table about replication with a black couple they have just met .
Denise Duplinski struggled to find words that left the feeling behind the performance .
&quot; It is hard to hear these terrible words and deeds ... coming from people who look like you and who do so because they look like you , &quot; she said .
&quot; What this really does with me makes me believe that the lynching continues , &quot; she added , &quot; today it is called only police shooting . &quot;
In the same room Tyrone Brooks court holds at his table , he eats and recalls the civil rights .
He said he had already started planning the performance next year .
&quot; The replication is in a way inspiring , because it describes the horror of what we have been suffering , but it is also a moment of learning , &quot; he said .
&quot; It is so if we don &apos;t learn anything from it , then it can happen again .
Those who do not study their own history , those who don &apos;t know their own history , are condemned to repeat it . &quot;
&quot; Death of the Gods &quot; the Wagnerians in Bayreuth reconciled themselves .
In the fourth part of Richard Wagner &apos;s monumental work , director Frank Castorf also sets his mind on a colourful and frivolous staging in a gigantic six @-@ story setting .
The rotary stage ( designed by Aleksandar Denic ) displays a shabby street corner with Berlin &apos;s Berlin Wall and Dönerbude , a &quot; Plaste und Elaste &quot; logo from the GDR &apos;s times or the New York Stock Exchange .
