New results of Swedish Asset Age Assessment Tests published
Recent results of tests for asylum seekers in Sweden , which have been subjected to a new medical age assessment method , show that more than 80 % of the people tested are older than 18 years , but not everyone believes in the accuracy of these results .
The Swedish Forensic Medicine Authority ( Rättsmedicinalverket ) has started to conduct the tests last year .
The purpose of the study is to make the evaluation of age more accurate during the asylum procedure , after the Swedish Immigration Office ( Migration Box ) was criticized for its mistakes in assessing the correct age of some refugees who were under the age of 18 .
So far Migration Offers have sent 6,880 cases to be tested and the Forensic Medicine Agency has published the results of a total of 2,481 tests from mid March to 31 July 2017 .
Eighty percent ( 2002 ) were assessed as 18 or older , while in 25 further cases the Forensic Medicine Agency assessed that the person was &quot; possibly 18 years or older . &quot;
In 432 cases the conclusion was that the person was &quot; possibly under the age of 18 . &quot;
The tests are only carried out in cases where the age of the person is called into question .
According to Johan Göransson of the Forensic Medicine Agency , the results have been consistent over the past months .
&quot; We don &apos;t see much difference in the statistics of the past months , the numbers are quite constant , &quot; he said to The Local magazine .
The high proportion of cases in which the person was assessed as being over 18 does not necessarily mean that the applicant lied about his or her age .
In May , the agency published the results of 581 tests and said that there were 442 cases that &quot; propose that the person being investigated was 18 years or older . &quot;
When the newspaper Svenska Dagbladet investigated the 581 published results , she found that in 243 of these cases the person had openly said that he was 18 years old this year .
The method of medical age assessment , in which the X @-@ ray recordings of wisdom teeth and MRI recordings of knee joints are made , which are then analysed by dentists and radiologists , has also been criticized in some areas .
A skeptic is the endocrinologist Claude Marcus of the Karolinska Institute , whose work includes assessing the biological maturity of people to see whether they passed through puberty sooner or later than usual .
&quot; I had reason to think about it and I do not think that the way of measuring should be used .
To assess biological age in this way is very uncertain .
If we cannot say it safely , we should not pretend we could , &quot; he told the Metro newspaper .
However , Göransson from the Forensic Medicine Agency defended the accuracy of tests : &quot; In general , you can say that the risk of judging a child as an adult is low .
The biggest risk is that the person is just under 18 , in this case about 10 % . &quot;
Fully on schedule
