The Bank of England will keep interest rates at 0.25 % .
The Bank of England is expected to announce that interest rates will remain unchanged after weak economic growth and inflation weakening have dampened speculation about an increase .
The Bank of England warned that economic growth would remain &quot; sluggish &quot; as it kept interest rates on family incomes in the midst of growing pressure .
The Bank &apos;s Monetary Committee ( MPC ) political decision @-@ makers voted 6 @-@ 2 to keep interest rates at 0.25 % , with fewer members this month seeking an increase because of the weak economic growth that weakened support for an increase .
In its quarterly inflation report , the Bank reduced its growth forecast to 1.7 % in 2017 and 1.6 % in 2018 , warning against continuing pressure on household income , with inflation expected to rise to close to 3 % in the autumn .
But she signalled that interest rate increases would be necessary in the coming years to control the inflation fuelled by the &quot; Brexiteers &quot; movement and said that the cost of credit could have to rise more than expected in the financial markets .
Members also voted to withdraw part of the Mammut economic stimulus package , which was issued one year after the end of the EU &apos;s presidency .
She will ask for time for the financing plan to provide cheaper financial resources for banks from February , although it was said that she is now expected to provide £ 15 billion more under this system - at £ 115 billion .
In the minutes after the decision on interest rates , the Bank said : &quot; In the MPC &apos;s central forecast , GDP will remain weak in the short term , as pressure on real household incomes continues to weigh on consumption . &quot;
At interest rates , she repeated that &quot; a certain tightening of monetary policy &quot; was necessary to cool inflation and a &quot; little greater &quot; dimension than the markets expect .
The markets are currently forecasting the first increase in the third quarter of next year and another in 2020 .
The Bank stressed , however , that increases would be &quot; gradual &quot; and &quot; limited . &quot;
The bank &apos;s downward revised growth forecasts for this year and next year are in comparison with the 1.9 % and 1.7 % forecast in May .
It has maintained its growth forecast to 1.8 % in 2018 .
The pound fell after these messages against the dollar and the euro .
Pounds fell by 0.5 % to $ 1.31 and fell by 0.4 % to € 1.11 .
The decision does not change after the recent disappointing growth figures dampened expectations of an interest rate increase and GDP rose from 0.2 % in the previous three months to 0.3 % in the second quarter .
Growth in the third quarter is expected to be 0.3 % , although the bank is expected to recover slightly towards the end of the year .
The latest inflation report showed little enthusiasm for households , which are affected by rising inflation and slight salary increases , as the tightening gets worse first before it gets better .
She added that monetary policy cannot &quot; prevent &quot; a drop in incomes in the coming years , but added that wages will recover &quot; significantly &quot; at the end of its three @-@ year forecast .
The economy is also strengthened by the rising demand for British goods , which is due to the weak pound , which will compensate for a portion of the lower consumption expenditure .
The decision comes one year after the lowering of interest rates to 0.25 % last August , after the shocking EU vote that caused the pound to sink .
The Bank estimates that it will probably take four years for this to fully reach prices and the economy .
Before the Bank &apos;s last decision on interest rates , the call for a rate increase had increased , as inflation increased pressure on hard @-@ pressed households , with three political decision @-@ makers demanding an increase of 0.5 % in July .
Inflation fell last month from 2.9 % in June to 2.6 % , although the bank said that this was expected and that it would pick up again in the months to come .
The bank said that the excess in relation to its 2 % target was &quot; all &quot; due to the weak pound .
Andrew Montlake , Coreco Mortgage Brokers Director , said : &quot; Given the recent decline in swap rates , the mortgage market should become more competitive again , as lenders are loudly looking for deals and want to end the year in a positive way .
The market looks particularly good for those who are leaving existing deals and making new mortgages , and many with free options and low rates are becoming more and more popular . &quot;
NHS ( The National Health Service ) is a report that controversial drug HIV prescribes 10,000 people .
Patients will be granted access to HIV &quot; transformative &quot; treatment from September after a High Court fight against NHS funding for the drug .
NHS England announced that a pre @-@ sexual preexposition prophylactic ( PrEP ) , which can reduce the risk of high @-@ risk infections by about 86 percent , is now being delivered by the health service via a first three @-@ year study for an estimated 10,000 @-@ strong population .
He agreed to fund the trial after the Court of Appeal upheld a Supreme Court ruling last year , according to which the NHS had the power to pay PrEP for it despite the claim that the local authorities should finance it .
The move announced on Thursday was welcomed by NHS England boss Simon Stevens as &quot; an important new intervention , &quot; while the National AIDS Trust ( NAT ) called this &quot; a decisive moment . &quot;
Sexual health clinics in London , Brighton , Manchester , Liverpool and Sheffield will be among the first to allow people to register , and further clinics will follow in October , before it is introduced throughout England in April 2018 .
Preexposition prophylactic ( PrEP ) is a consequence of HIV preparations taken before sex in order to reduce the risk of HIV infection .
Studies have shown that PrEP significantly reduces HIV transmission in case of infection risk .
Taking PrEP before sexual intercourse means that there are just enough medicines in a person &apos;s body to prevent HIV infection .
Such medications can be taken every day or so already two hours before sexual intercourse .
The treatment is for gay or bisexual men , transsexuals and people with HIV @-@ positive partners who have not been successfully treated .
Clinics identify suitable participants who agree with the study , including men , women , transgenders and individuals who have a partner whose HIV status is not known to be controlled by antiretroviral treatment .
People who live in England and are registered with a family doctor may also register for a possible participation in their local participating sex clinic .
The program will be £ 10 million and NHS England signed a contract to deliver the medications this week after an international competitive procurement .
The study is intended to assess PrEP &apos;s entire potential by gathering clinical evidence as it is specifically used , received and implemented on a large scale .
Stevens said : &quot; This major new intervention should complement and overtake the far @-@ reaching and increasingly successful efforts to prevent HIV .
It is another milestone in the progress that has been made for more than three decades in meeting one of the greatest health challenges facing humanity . &quot;
Deborah Gold , Chief Executive of NAT ( National AIDS Trust ) , said : &quot; This is a decisive moment in the fight against HIV .
PrEP , if it is targeted at the needy and vulnerable , offers the opportunity to change the English HIV epidemic .
From September onwards , people at high risk of HIV will have access to this NHS @-@ financed attempt in England to develop a new instrument that is truly individually controlled and not subject to negotiation with a partner , which will improve many , many lives .
We warmly welcome this announcement . &quot;
Ian Green , president of the Terrence Higgins Trust , said : &quot; The priority now must be to make the study fast throughout the country and to leave nobody at risk of HIV behind .
Now that PrEP has been purchased , we are on the way to protecting more than 10,000 people infected with HIV . &quot;
Sharon Hodgson , shadows minister of health said : &quot; The start of the PrEP process is welcome and overdue after months of government delay and hesitant implementation .
The evidence shows how this drug can be transformative as part of our approach to HIV prevention and how it can end the transmission of this life @-@ changing infection .
This attempt brings us a step closer to understanding the advantages of PrEP .
It is now important that this study is carried out as soon as possible throughout the country to protect people who are HIV infected and to help us to get a step closer to ending HIV spread in society .
Living the big life : meandering floors and clever designer tricks have turned this little Earls Court apartment into a spacious home .
Joanne Leigh , a former banker with a passion for real estate , shrunk in 2014 from a large house in Knightsbridge to an Earls Court apartment .
It is located on the first floor of an elegant Queen Anne Terrace - and it is proof of the new design that Joanne has not packed and sold her bags .
&quot; Before that , I lived with my ex in Knightsbridge in a much larger room , &quot; Joanne said .
&quot; I was looking for something that was comparable to my price .
This apartment was shown to me in Earls Court and she had the same high ceilings .
I fell in love with her and made an offer on that day . &quot;
Since the first visit the 1,000 square foot flat has been completely reconstructed .
Originally a one @-@ room property with a nested layout - you had to walk through the kitchen to get into the bedroom - Joanne wanted to add storage space and an intermediate floor to make the most of the generous ceiling height .
&quot; I wanted high ceilings , large windows , lots of light .
That was my requirement , &quot; she says .
For this purpose , she commissioned Erfan Azadi Architecture and Design Studio Duck &amp; Shed from Notting Hill to use the 12.5 @-@ foot high ceilings and create a home that was not only a development but also a place to live for Joanne .
The ceiling was not large enough to place two rooms above each other , so the architectural challenge was to create a mezzanine floor where one could stand .
Erfan had to think creatively to solve the problem .
&quot; The steelmaking plant is rather complicated , &quot; he explains .
&quot; We had to arrange the rooms in such a way that they were stacked one over another without having to be assembled .
It was not just a matter of building a simple platform , but of putting the steel in a way that it had the height for the function above or below . &quot;
This arrangement allowed Erfan to place two rooms at full height above each other - with a much more drama .
For the mezzanine floor , a classic Retrouvius copper @-@ based light window looks down over the glamorous residential area .
The style of the programme is based on gold furniture , silver accents and vintage Mouille lighting .
The use of mirrors plays a role in the addition of theatres , space and light .
A short corridor is mirrored on both ends to make it look longer .
In the kitchen , the mirror @-@ like window looks like a window into another room .
At the entrance , mirrored units can host all Joanne &apos;s cutlery and glasses .
By perceiving a glamorous space , the beauty of the living room can close the kitchen if it is not used .
Folding kitchens are nothing new .
The special feature of this door is that it is a garage door .
&quot; A bit of engineering art went in , &quot; explains Erfan of the steel @-@ reinforced , furious MDF door .
&quot; It is counterbalanced , so that when you push the door up , it glides very easily . &quot;
Thanks to the clever use of space and storage space , Joanne was able to gain an additional 16 m ² , an additional bedroom and an improved layout .
&quot; At the end of the day you will sell the land or how your property is valued , &quot; Erfan says .
&quot; In a rudimentary form a real estate agent will enter and perform a calculation based on the size of the ground . &quot;
Here they have managed to increase the floor surface and at the same time use high @-@ quality materials that give the room its luxurious advantage .
&quot; The materials are the things you touch and that therefore help to create an emotional bond in a home , &quot; Erfan says .
&quot; You don &apos;t fall in love with the electric socket in the walls .
These are important , but they do not play with your heart . &quot;
It shows that a flat can turn into something amazing by means of a good design and a higher square meter of something standard .
In fact , as Joanne , you could decide not to leave this .
Brothers fight for a $ 500 million real estate empire
Two brothers , four decades after their Italian family founded the dynasty , are in a dispute over one of Victorias &apos; largest real estate empires , valued at over $ 500 million .
Colin De Lutis , former director of the Carlton Football Club , lives in a villa in Toorak , just three doors from his younger brother Paul , who brought him to the Supreme Court to clarify how his property and his business assets should be shared .
Paul wanted to withdraw from the family business as early as 2014 , but a sharp dispute about the separation takes place in the Supreme Court .
Together with their mother Anna , the brothers own 38 properties valued at more than half a billion dollars , accumulated since 1973 the family bought a shop in Smith Street , Collingwood .
There the Westco Jeans family founded and grew into a chain of 160 retail stores .
It was sold in 1999 for 85 million dollars .
De Lutis has invested in real estate , and its portfolio now includes offices in the city centre , the development areas of Brunswick and Sorrento , the Gold Coast and Mt Buller apartments , and suburban shopping centres .
According to an agreement with his deceased father , Colin - described by Paul as &quot; the main operator and the dominant personality &quot; in the De Lutis family - owns about two @-@ thirds of the company &apos;s assets and Paul about one @-@ third .
A court decree states that this means that Colin owns property valued at 316 million dollars and Paul owns 176 million dollars .
The division of real estate and cash has proved to be a problem for Paul .
&quot; Colin &apos;s proposed divisions left him with what I thought to be the best objects , &quot; he wrote in a first Affidavit in 2015 .
Last Friday , after several failed brokering , the first hearing of the Supreme Court took place .
The brothers are now also fighting for control of the existing business - something that Colin claimed to have excluded him .
Colin argues that this is not true , but accuses Paul of having incompetently manage some of his possessions .
In an early affidavit filed by Colin , he said he hoped to resolve the dispute .
&quot; My Italian heritage dictates that the family is the most important priority , &quot; he wrote .
Costs rise in this case , with hundreds of pages of affairs , emails and reports from companies , including Deloitte , Pitcher Partners and Keck Cramer , and leading lawyers , including Allan Myers , QC , and law firms held by both parties .
Mrs. De Lutis and her deceased husband Luigi were Italian immigrants who operated dairy bars and confectionery shops from the late 1950 &quot; s and bought a shop in Brunswick in the early 1970s .
They used the security to buy the Collingwood estate , where Colin opened the family &apos;s first jeans shop .
Colin says that he started there when he &quot; was in my last years of high school , when Paul was still a little child ( about 10 years old ) . &quot;
Anna De Lutis says that she and Luigi , who died in 2012 , hoped to pass on their property to their sons amicably .
&quot; Luigi often said to me that he never wanted the brothers to land in court , &quot; she wrote .
Two other brothers , Mark and Luke , are no longer part of the De Lutis group , Colin claims that Mark paid 10 million dollars and Luke 26 million dollars when he left the company .
Both Lukas and Mark were , Paul said , &quot; embittered about the conditions of their financial separation . &quot;
Colin has stayed very quiet , even though he was a former AFL board member and the best friend of the television star after a biography by Eddie McGuire in 2015 .
Fairfax Media established contacts with Colin and Paul De Lutis and their lawyers .
Only Colin responded via SMS to say &quot; I really can not say much , &quot; because the case was before the courts .
&quot; It is very , very sad that my brother Paul has decided to sue me and to take our surname through the courts , &quot; he wrote .
In mid @-@ 2016 , Colin wrote to the Court in a letter to the effect that he had tried to fulfill the demands of his brother .
&quot; Our father didn &apos;t want Paul and I to end up in our current dispute , &quot; he said and added that he &quot; still hoped for a solution . &quot;
On Friday , this hope seemed to have passed .
&quot; Our matter will be solved in the near future by the courts , &quot; Colin wrote .
Trumps &apos;s new Chief of Staff plans to limit the president &apos;s lack of media coverage .
Others tried and failed .
Maybe John F. Kelly can do it .
If so , he will be the first .
Politico reports that the new Chief of Staff of the White House is planning to limit the flow of information to President Bush - including news reports - in the hope of keeping the boss on a more even path .
Here is a piece by reporter Josh Dawsey :
&quot; When John F. Kelly , the White House &apos;s new Chief of Staff , met senior executives on his first working day , he posed a major problem to President Donald Trumps White House that he wanted to repair : poor information coming into the hands of the President .
Kelly told the staff that information about him should flow - whether on paper or in briefings - because the president would make better decisions if he received good information . &quot;
Kell &apos;s diagnosis makes sense , but others have tried to tame him by monitoring his media diet .
President of Trumps &apos;s relationship to television dates back decades - and now that he is in the White House , his television advertising is still strong .
&quot; If candidate Tom was angry about an unfair reporting , it was productive to show him that he was getting a fair report from the convincing , &quot; said ex @-@ campaign consultant Sam Nunberg in February to Politico .
Politicos Tara Palmeri wrote at the time that &quot; the key to keeping Trumps Twitter in control , according to six former election officials , is to ensure that his personal media consumption involves a steady praise . &quot;
Okay .
But the idea that Trumps has ever been &quot; under control &quot; in Twitter is ridiculous .
Perhaps these campaign officers know something we don &apos;t know to others - that without their interventions , Trumps tweets would have contained even more ignition .
We will probably never hear about the tweets that he didn &apos;t send .
If his colleagues have managed to keep him away from him a few times before , their efforts have been rewarded .
But nobody has consistently managed to prevent a controversy from brewing by a party like that of a man .
Part of the problem is that in a White House composed of competing parties , people try to push their own agenda by presenting a material that might promote their views from the world .
Politico - concerned with this story - reported in May on the advisors &apos; preference to provide the president with dubious information for strategic reasons .
This was an example described by reporter Shane Goldmacher :
&quot; Current and former staff members of the committee say that he can respond like a volcano to negative press cuts , especially those with embarrassing leaks , and that he is stiffened to find out where they came from .
This is exactly what happened at the end of February , when someone maliciously gave the president a copy of an article written by GotNews.com , the website of the internet provocateur Charles C. Johnson , who accused Deputy Chief of Katie Walsh of being &quot; the source behind a bunch of leaks &quot; in the White House .
Although Johnson was permanently banned from Twitter because of bullying , or he did not offer any concrete evidence , or that he had made false accusations in the past and revocated them .
He read the article and started to question staff about Walsh . &quot;
Goldmaker added that Reince Priebus , Chief of Staff at the time , and the Secretary General of the White House , Rob Porter , &quot; attempted to implement a system to manage and document the paperwork that he receives . &quot;
How did it work ?
Kelly tries to do the same three months later .
Perhaps he will prove himself to be more effective manager than priebus , but he is still his impulsive self and his advisors still strive for influence .
These unchangeable factors make Kelly &apos;s mission very difficult .
