Russia affair : US Special Attorney sets up Grand Jury .
In the Russia affair involving US President Donald Trump , Special Enlightener Robert Mueller is alleged to have appointed a so @-@ called Grand Jury , according to a report .
The panel has started work in the past weeks , wrote the Wall Street Journal calling on familiar persons to review the case .
The newspaper regarded the step as a sign that Mueller had expanded and intensified his investigation .
CNN reported that the panel had already made initial submissions in connection with a meeting between the oldest son of Trumps and a Russian lawyer .
US intelligence agencies have been accusing the Kremlin of having engaged in the presidential campaign for a number of years in the year 2016 .
Mueller investigates whether there were any agreements between the Trumps campaign team and Moscow .
The investigation will focus on several men who were active as Republican counselor during the campaign .
It is reported that Mueller has also been increasingly looking at the private and business environment of Trumps .
The Washington Post wrote a few weeks ago that the Special Enquiry had put into the center of the case the special enquirer , Mr Trump , and investigated the accusation that the president might have hindered the judiciary in the course of the affair .
Grand Jurys &quot; main task is to examine the evidence in possible criminal cases and to decide whether to make a charge .
Grand Jurys are therefore also called chambers of indictment .
They are switched on most of the time when the cases are large and controversial .
The process is always done behind closed doors .
The panel may also be consulted by the Prosecutor &apos;s Office to bring persons under oath for interrogation .
CNN has already issued subpoenas that address the meeting between Donald Trump Jr. and a Russian lawyer .
People who participated in the meeting were invited to make a statement , the report said .
They should also provide documents .
In addition to the award of the prize to a number of Trumps , the interview was attended by his counsel and son @-@ in @-@ law Jared Kushner and his former campaign chairman Paul Manafort .
On Thursday evening , legal experts said on US television that the appointment showed that Mueller was not intimidated in his work and that he wanted to act quickly if necessary .
However , it should not necessarily mean that the Grand Jury will be activated at the end to decide on the collection of an accusation .
Nevertheless , the appointment of the committee was the start of a new phase of investigation and a new cause for concern for President Bush .
In the midst of the new revelations , the president used a speech in West Virginia on Thursday evening to portray the Russia affair as a campaign of his political opponents .
&quot; The history of Russia is a total invention , &quot; said Mr. Trumpf .
The subject gives the democrats a chance to &quot; feel good , because they have nothing else to talk about . &quot;
In a statement , his special advisor Ty Cobb explained that he knew nothing about the appointment of a Grand Jury .
But he also emphasized that they usually work in secret .
The White House is in favour of any measure that &quot; fairly &quot; accelerates the work of the Special Encounter .
James Comey , former FBI chief , said three times that he was not going to be prosecuted against the president .
There is no reason to believe that something has changed , the statement goes on .
Mueller had only been used after he fired Comey .
Because of this accusation of expulsion , he had been accused of having wanted to hamper justice .
Comey told a Senate committee in June that he had asked him to stop investigating the then national security advisor Michael Flynn .
The President is under great pressure for the investigation .
According to reports from the US media , the White House collected material against the Special Encounter and its employees .
This led to speculations as to whether he would dismiss Mueller .
Unlike an independent congress special researcher , such as in the Watergate affair involving the later resigning president Richard Nixon , Mueller has a position in which he could theoretically be fired by the Ministry of Justice at the request of the President .
Several senators from both parties want to prevent such a step .
Two groups of Republican and Democratic senators presented draft laws on Thursday , according to which the Special Envoy could no longer be dismissed so easily .
Antarctica : Gigantischer Eisberg is driving in Antarctica .
In the West Antarctic , a gigantic iceberg has freed itself from the shelding of Berlin , which is almost seven times larger than the city of Berlin .
The Bremerhavener Alfred Wegener Institute for Polar and Marine Research announced this on Wednesday .
The colossus , which weighs over a trillion tons and covers 5800 square kilometres , is now driving north and will probably take two or three years to melt .
With a length of 175 kilometres and a width of up to 50 kilometres , it is one of the largest ice giants registered by researchers in the last three decades .
He does not assume any danger to people .
The extent to which climate change has played a role in the breakdown of the iceberg is unclear according to researchers .
However , for about 30 years , a series of unusual collapse of ice @-@ elves has been in the region , says the US Research Center for Snow and Ice ( NSIDC ) in a background of facts .
In the opinion of many researchers , this could be related to global warming .
The now abandoned colossus was a part of the Larsen @-@ C shells .
Shelf is swimming on the sea and is fed by large glaciers .
It grows further and further into the sea and breaks down regularly .
Larsen @-@ C shells are located south of South America , almost at the top of the Antarctic Peninsula .
To the north of this ice @-@ skeleton the balance of growth and demolition was disturbed some years ago .
A complete shelding has been destroyed there , writes AWI researcher Daniela Jansen .
There is a reason to say that the larsen C shells are also in danger , because the front edge has never withdrawn as far as it is today .
In addition , the iron plate was under stress and could fall apart if a piece of the break @-@ off occurs at a key point .
The melting of the ice and the shells does not increase the sea level , as they swim on the water and ice displaces as much water as it produces after the melting itself .
However , the loss of the ice of the Schelfeise means that glaciers can flow directly into the sea and drain much faster than before .
This could possibly raise the sea level , but only to a small degree , writes the British research project on the observation of Antarctica ( Midas ) .
Larsen @-@ C shells are the fourth largest shells in Antarctica , with AWI data covering almost 50,000 square kilometres .
It is up to 700 metres thick at the transition from the mainland and about 200 metres thick at the front edge of the ocean .
There it stops regularly .
&quot; Although it is natural and we don &apos;t know of any direct connection to climate change , the Ice Sheet is now in a more vulnerable position , &quot; said Martin O &apos;Leary of Swansea University in the UK .
&quot; This is the widest withdrawal since records were recorded , &quot; he also confirmed .
Among scientists , it was disputed whether the Larsen @-@ C shells would now grow again or whether they would go back in the long term and possibly fall apart , said his colleague Adrian Luckman and added : &quot; Our models say that it will be less stable . &quot;
But this development will take decades .
Although these researchers do not trace the breakdown of this gigantic iceberg back to climate change , many ice @-@ skeletons of the Antarctic peninsula have collapsed in the past decades .
This long , Antarctic cider stretches towards South America .
According to data from the British Antarctic Survey , the ice surface of the Antarctic peninsula has fallen by 28,000 square kilometres since the 1950s .
The temperature increase had contributed to the decline .
The Antarctic peninsula has warmed by about three degrees in the last 50 years , making it one of the areas where the temperature is rising the fastest .
