Lavender from Provence threatened by climate change
It has become a symbol of Provence in the south of France , but there is a warning that it is threatened by climate change .
The lavender is in decline due to the conditions reminiscent of drought and the insects transmitted by disease , which is a major headache for scientists and farmers who are trying to find solutions .
Eric Chaisse , expert of CRIEPPAM , the agricultural research institute in Provence , said : &quot; Lavender in Provence is effectively endangered because the plant has been exposed to the effects of climate change in recent years , which has brought far fewer rainfalls in the spring and much more in the autumn .
This has an impact on the life of lavender .
There is also a small cicade , a microbacterium that transmits dwarf cages , and in very dry weather there are many of them . &quot;
And there is a lot at stake .
Lava oil does not only attract holidaymakers , it is also very popular for perfumes and cosmetics .
Eric Chaisse said : &quot; Instead of having very long plant watches with a large number of flowers , we have smaller , underdeveloped and either missing or weakened .
So we have fewer parts with essential oil . &quot;
The solutions that are being considered include better water distribution and slowing down evaporation from the soil , spraying plants with a protective mixture of clay and water , and developing varieties that are more resistant to dryness .
