Dinosaurs used camouflage to evade their enemies .
At first glance , it looks like a statue , though a terrifying one .
Researchers have now found that the first insights of the incredible rhino of a Canadian miner have been preserved so well that experts have described it as the &quot; Mona Lisa the the Dinosaur . &quot;
It was so well preserved that they could even notice that his scaly skin was from Roman brown - and they said it had something of a &quot; troubled past . &quot;
The report in Current Biology magazine called it &quot; the best preserved armored dinosaur ever found and one of the best dinosaur specimens in the world . &quot;
The fossil is a newly discovered species of nodosaurians who lived in the mid @-@ caves between 110 million and 112 million years ago .
His incredibly good condition has astonished researchers who describe it as &quot; really remarkable . &quot;
According to reports in National Geographic , the armored herbivore is the best preserved fossil of its kind .
It was found by Shawn Funk when he excavated the Suncor Millenium Mine on March 21 , 2011 near Fort McMurray in the north of Alberta , Canada .
He met something that seemed to be out of place with the surrounding rock , and decided to look more closely .
Fossil , discovered by him , was sent to the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Paleontology .
They spent the next six years exploring the wild animal within the 2500 pound ( 1,100 kg ) heavy earth clump .
After all this hard work , the final result is now ready to be revealed .
&quot; If you just blindly blink a little with your eyes , you might almost believe that it has slept , &quot; said the main author Caleb Brown , scientist in the Royal Tyrrell Museum where the creature is visible .
&quot; It will enter the history of science as one of the most beautiful and best preserved dinosaur specimens - the Mona Lisa the dinosaurs . &quot;
Researchers have found that this herbivoe , although covered with armor and resembling a changing tank , was probably threatened by meats @-@ eating dinosaurs .
The heavily armored dinosaur used a red and white camouflage to hide from predators and used a shield technique known as countershade , which is also used by many modern animals .
This would have allowed him to mix better in his surroundings and to escape his enemies , experts say , suggesting that it was threatened by larger dinosaurs .
The 110 million year old creature from the Nodosaurian family first made headlines in May and is now available at the Royal Tyrrell Museum of Palaeontology .
Researchers have now studied the wild animal and named it Borealopelta markmitchelli , after the museum technician Mark Mitchell , one of a team that spent more than 7,000 hours carefully revealing it .
The amazing conservation of the copy enabled Dr. Mitchell and an international team of scientists to document the pattern and shape of dandruff and armor on his body .
They discovered the conqueror shading , a common defensive method in the animal kingdom , which means that the top of the creature was darker than its bottom .
Although the counter @-@ shading is common , the results are surprising , as the size of Borealopelta is far greater than the size of the animals living today .
It suggests that the dinosaur was hunted by sufficient pressure from meat @-@ eating dinosaurs , that evolution favoured the veiling of the confrontation as a means of survival .
But most contemporary animals that use the conqueror - remember to think of deer , zebras or belly animals - are much smaller and more vulnerable than prey , which indicates that this nodosaurian was exposed to a real struggle for survival .
&quot; Strong predators behaviour on a massive , heavily armored dinosaur shows how dangerous the dinosaur predators of the time of creation must have been , &quot; said Brown .
Chemical analysis of organic compounds in their scales also shows that the dinosaur skin was reddish brown .
Scientists are also investigating the animal for evidence of its life , including its preserved intestines , to find out what it had eaten as its last meal .
They believe that when the dinosaur died , he was put into a river and driven out into the sea where he sank on his back to the sea bottom .
At that time Alberta was as warm as Südflorida today , and rivers and oceans are likely to spread far inland than they are today .
&quot; This noodle sucker is really remarkable because it is completely covered with preserves scaly skin , but also conserves three dimensions and conserves the original shape of the animal , &quot; said Brown .
&quot; The result is that the animal looks almost exactly the same today as it did in the early time of creation .
You don &apos;t need to use much imagination to reconstruct it , and if you only have to look a little with your eyes , you might almost believe that it is sleeping . &quot;
The time of the creation was when huge theropods , carnivorous dinosaurs standing on two legs , crossed the earth .
Although the king of Tyrannosaurus Rex lived millions of years after Borealopelta , the armored dinosaur was hunted by some of his frightening ancestors .
This includes the Acrocanthosaurus , a 38 @-@ foot ( 11.5 m ) long six @-@ ton heavy monster .
Researchers whose latest findings are published in the Current Biology journal believe that Borealopelta was sunk in the sea after death and mumbled in the mud .
The creature was found by Shawn Funk when he excavated it in the Suncor Millenium Mine on 21 March 2011 near Fort McMurray in the north of Alberta , Canada .
According to the museum , it is the best preserved armored dinosaur in the world , including skin and armor , and is complete from the muzzle to the hips .
The creatures were on average about 18 feet ( 5 meters ) long and weighed up to 3.000 pounds ( 1,300 kg ) .
It contained two 20 @-@ inch lace tips that raged from his shoulders .
Researchers believe that the armored herbivore was washed through western Canada until a flooded river flooded it into the open sea .
But the underwater treatment of the dinosaur preserved his armor in exquisite detail .
The fossilized remains of this particular specialty are preserved so well that the remains of the skin still cover wood @-@ fired armour plates along the dinosaur .
As Michael Greshko wrote for National Geographic , such a level of conservation is as rare as a lottery win .
The more I look at it , the more confusing it will be .
Stoneyed remains of the skin still cover the wood panic plates that carry the skull of the animal .
His right forefoot is on his side , his five fingers are uplifted .
I can count the scales on his sole &apos; .
