The St. Louis rapper was in the process of tweeting live the death of Michael Brown , an unarmed black 18 @-@ year @-@ old , who was killed by a white police officer in Ferguson , Mon .
He posted an image of Brown &apos;s lifeless body , which had spread out on the street and would stay there for four hours .
&quot; That &apos;s wild , &quot; Ferrell thought with himself .
But death was not new for them and their community .
She put her mobile phone off to return to tweets about people on the Canfield Drive later in the evening .
There were photos of the police band and people shouting , and of a man who claimed to be Brown &apos;s father , who carried a sign saying : &quot; Ferguson &apos;s police just killed my unarmed son ! &quot;
She watched a live feed , where a policeman stood in front of a group of demonstrators with a beaking dog .
In another video , she said , another policeman seized his gun and shouted : &quot; I &apos;ll kill you ... animals all . &quot;
&quot; This is not the usual way , &quot; Ferrell recalled her statement .
&quot; This is not the kind of murder we have become accustomed to and which we can call typical . &quot;
At that moment I felt chills .
I was furious and wept . &quot;
She booked a return flight and went home the following day .
After landing at 11 p.m. , she picked up her 6 @-@ year @-@ old daughter from her father &apos;s house and decided that the two would go to Ferguson the next morning .
What happened in the following three years with Ferrell and Ferguson is the theme of a new documentary film , &quot; Whose Streets ? , &quot; in the cinemas starting on 11 .
It is a story of survival and protest , love and loss , strength and toughness , which consists of the view of the people who live in the community and who have filled the streets to demand answers .
When Ferrell traveled to Ferguson with her daughter , who was wearing a flower dress and a suitable crown , they discussed a conversation they had countless times before this morning about the black experience and the black condition .
&quot; You remember how I taught you , when black people have to fight for what they believe ? &quot; she remembered what she said .
&quot; We are now going to Ferguson because the police killed an 18 @-@ year @-@ old boy and that was not correct .
I couldn &apos;t take them with me , &quot; Ferrell said later .
&quot; What happened today is a high point of what happened in the past .
She has to know it and see it and be raised in it .
It must be well developed , not only to know activism and organization , but also the different layers of black experience and black constitution , and what we must do to become free , to liberate ourselves .
Although lucky to give her an experience that does not personally experience blatant racism , she is no different from the next black woman child .
I feel I would do her a bad service to keep her away from it . &quot;
The first station , when they arrived in Ferguson , was at the South Florissant Road , a site opposite the police authority that became a campground when the activists were waiting for the possible non @-@ prosecution of Officer Darren Wilson .
There Ferrell and her daughter were connected with a local business owner who made lunch .
They prepared their bags and helped to pass them on to demonstrators .
Slowly , a new kind of activism for Ferrell began to take shape .
&quot; My activism has changed completely after August 9 , &quot; she said and noticed that as a student at Missouri @-@ St. Louis University , she was president of the Minority Student Nurses Association and focused on food justice and health inequalities in black communities .
&quot; I had no experience in organizing .
Everything has come . &quot;
Weeks later Ferrell met during an evening demonstration Sabaah Folayan , one of the co @-@ directors of the film .
Folayan said that she and her cameraman Lucas Alvarado @-@ Farrar just wanted to &quot; document &quot; what happened .
Ferrell came to mind a question : &quot; Do you want to document or try to find a story you can exploit ? &quot;
This skepticism was nourished by innumerable cases of people , often whites , in a community that was profited from their pain and their resilience .
As Folayan recently stated in an interview : &quot; We had heard that people who were born and raised in Ferguson didn &apos;t centered their voices and we wanted to do it differently . &quot;
Folayan had already worked with the then co @-@ director Damon Davis , an artist from the field who is well @-@ known for his activism around death @-@ stract @-@ inmates .
Ferrell brought the project closer , and together with six other locals , the Whose Streets team followed in the years following Brown &apos;s shootings .
&quot; This documentary is not someone who speaks for us or who speaks with us . &quot; We are talking , &quot; Davis said .
&quot; That was the main thing for me , how these people are depicted , because that is how I will be shown . &quot;
The goal was not to concentrate on Ferrell &apos;s experience - to show that the movement was not &quot; a Messiah leader , but a community that comes together , &quot; said Folayan , who proved to be the most open participant in the film .
As a result , documents from &quot; Whose Streets ? &quot; show surprisingly personal aspects of Ferrells life as she falls in love and marries her wife .
&quot; I felt like naked , &quot; Ferrell said , laughing when she first saw the finished product .
&quot; But I understand that there must be a certain vulnerability in this work and in the humanization of black people . &quot;
In addition , Ferrells story repeats the role of black gay women in the broader black @-@ lives @-@ matter movement .
Two of the three founders of the movement , who marked the film &quot; BlacklivesMatter &quot; in social media after the killing of black teenager Trayvon Martin in 2012 , identify themselves as gay .
&quot; Black , gay women in executive positions have supported the movement as a whole , &quot; said Ferrell , &quot; and that is why we are constantly fighting on different fronts .
Black , gay women have to carry the main burden of everything . &quot;
Folayan agreed to note that &quot; the movement is really maintained by black women and many black women . &quot;
She insists , however , that &quot; this is not an affirmative type of action , &quot; for which Ferrell was chosen for its identity as the heart of the film .
&quot; She was the person who aroused this energy , &quot; she said .
&quot; It is not a coincidence , though she was at the front of the scene , because so much is at stake in a life at these crossings as a black gay woman .
You can feel your energy through the screen . &quot;
Meanwhile , the news cameras in Ferguson have long since disappeared , but the racial connections between the community and the police still exist .
Ferrell is on probation for 2 ½ years because of the blocking of a highway during a demonstration .
She describes the social @-@ political atmosphere of the area she calls her home : &quot; A Chaos . &quot;
Yet she clings to a song and feels bound to this memory as she took to the streets in Ferguson three years ago .
&quot; It is our duty to fight for our freedom .
It is our duty to win .
We must love each other and support each other .
We have nothing to lose - except our chains . &quot;
From &quot; Dream &quot; to &quot; Size @-@ like &quot; : TV readers discuss bit gallery .
The bit gallery moves people , although it is not built yet .
But the idea of a shopping centre on the southern end of the pedestrian zone has been dividing the population into advocates and opponents since the first presentation in 2009 .
The topic does not leave much of a cold .
The topic is also discussed on our Facebook page &quot; Volksfreund Bitburg . &quot;
While some say to the bit gallery : &quot; That &apos;s a dream &quot; ( Anja Becker ) or &quot; That &apos;s really great &quot; ( Nicole Schausten ) , the other side comes from : &quot; Need no man &quot; ( Siggi Becken ) or simply &quot; Grandmage ! &quot; ( Lars Scholz ) .
While the advocates believe that one gallery will attract more customers to the city , which will also benefit the other shops , the opponents fear that the pedestrian zone will be bluegged .
