The start @-@ ups that will help your company not to become the next user .
Amy Errett wanted to measure the luck of your employees in your e @-@ commerce start @-@ up , but the surveys did not work .
Answers were often vague , unhelpful or - even worse - fraudulent .
And even if you promised anonymity , some workers didn &apos;t have the courage to go to trial .
&quot; There was simply never consistency and objectivity , &quot; said Errett , who operates the 75 @-@ person San Francisco e @-@ Commerce Hair Care Company Madison Reed .
She has called people from outside for help .
A new race of Human Resources Start @-@ ups is growing to help companies find out how their employees feel .
By creating and licensing software that has the specific purpose of measuring employee commitment , it enables companies to conduct surveys , to address specific groups and demographics groups , to provide anonymity and complaint hotlines to employees , and in some cases to allow whistleblowers to bypass C @-@ Suite managers and to directly approach the board .
&quot; You now have tools like Strava and Fitbit to track your health , but where is the Fitbit for your company ? &quot; said Jim Barnett , co @-@ founder and chief executive of Redwood City Start @-@ up Glint , whose software analytics tools are used by companies to measure employee commitment .
Say that they won a better insight into what your employees think and feel with Glint in the three years .
In addition to the snap surveys and surveys of specific teams , it also features a heat map of the company , which shows at a glance which units have the most complaints and which managers have low approval results - so that you can follow up on why .
Companies come to understand that you must stay at the top of the workplace culture so that you don &apos;t become the next person involved in scandals , since a former employee has published a blog post describing an environment of harassment that penalizes those who have spoken .
For start @-@ ups like Glint , this wish is a lucrative business opportunity after an overview .
Global Governance , Regulation and Compliance could exceed $ 118.7 billion in value in 2020 , according to the financial website Let &apos;s Talk Payments .
Denver , Colorado , Start @-@ up Convercent , which helps companies prevent and recognize bad behavior , saw a growing interest and activity early this year in the midst of the Uber fall in discredit .
Convercent has almost 600 clients , including Airbnb , Microsoft and Tesla .
Uber has recently signed up as a client .
As Glint puts it , Convercent companies can send customized &quot; pulse &quot; surveys , gather confidential real @-@ time responses and view the heat maps of your problem areas .
It also provides an anonymous SMS hotline that allows employees to report bad behaviour .
And if the Chief Executive Officer is included , complaints go directly to the Supervisory Board .
&quot; The Court of Public Opinions has captured the regulators , &quot; said Patrick Quinlan , Convercent &apos;s founder and managing director .
When a company is found to treat its employees badly or behave unethical , it can experience costly consequences by consumer boycott , worker fluctuation and complaints , Quinlan said .
Ruby Tuesday , the restaurant chain with more than 25,000 employees and over 500 locations , has deployed Convercent for more than a year to ensure that employees are aware of politics and procedures and have an easy way to reach their headquarters .
In the past , if an employee wanted to report a problem , he or she had to find a telephone number or email for the company headquarters , file a formal complaint and hope that he was taken seriously .
It was often an intimidation and uncomfortable experience , James Vitrano , Ruby Tuesdays General Counsel , who said there was no good way to track the complaints of employees .
Now that Convercent is using it , problems previously hidden in offices in cities or states - such as discrimination , harassment or unfair internships - can be identified and addressed faster .
&quot; I can get closer to this holistic 360 degree view in the experience of employment , &quot; said Vitrano , who is managing the Ruby Tuesday &apos;s risk management group .
&quot; And we protect our shareholders from employment disputes . &quot;
Companies started to take ethics , values and employee commitment more seriously in 2002 , after the accounting company Arthur Andersen collapsed because of ethical breaches by the Enron scandal , Quinlan said .
But it took until the &quot; Social Media came to their right &quot; that companies realized that they could not prevent their dirty laundry from going online .
&quot; Before using technology to monitor ethics , people used hope as a strategy , &quot; he said .
Both Glint and Convercent provide their software services and burden companies with recurring fees for using their products .
It is a business model and an opportunity that has the approval of Venture Capital investors who have supported both start @-@ ups .
Convercent received $ 10 million in February financing from companies such as Sapphire Ventures and Tola Capital , raising the total capital to $ 47 million .
In November , the company was able to secure $ 10 million from Bessemer Venture Partners and brought its total financing to $ 60 million .
These investments are hardly surprising given the networked nature of companies , culture and venture capital .
There is a growing body of research that shows that today &apos;s employees expect more from their jobs than they have done in the past .
In competitive markets such as Silicon Valley , high salaries and interesting projects are only used .
The employees want to feel that they are accepted and appreciated and that they are giving their time to a company with a positive mission .
&quot; If people are glad to be in a company that feels their voices belong , and that the work you do is worthwhile , then they feel more committed to making this company successful , &quot; said Nina McQueen , Vice President of Global Benefits and Employee Experience at LinkedIn , who uses both Convercent and Glint .
Investors achieve returns when their portfolio companies run well , and companies run well when employees are engaged and engaged .
If the analytics tools of third parties promise to increase the commitment and commitment of the employees , it is no wonder that you will find support .
The data on employee commitment according to the experts of the workplace culture important .
But the data is useless if the managers of a company do not take any action .
Indeed , if a company asks employees for their feedback , it may have an expectation that change is on its way .
And if change does not come fast enough , or not at all , it can breed disappointments and demotivate employees .
&quot; If you are looking for 4,000 ideas , you must be prepared to have 4,000 talks , &quot; said Russell Raath , President of Consulting at the Business Management Company Kotter International , who has seen companies that have relied too much on data collection .
&quot; For if you do not follow employees will ask themselves , &quot; Did you really hear me ?
Are you considering it ?
And who you don &apos;t care about , why should I ? &quot;
Madison Reed , who is now conducting monthly employee surveys , was able to take action on the same day when a problem is reported about Glint , says Errett .
And after collecting feedback from frustrated employees , it was able to reorganize several teams within the company by the speed of decision making and the quality of communication , and to add a communication training to address the problems .
At Ruby Tuesday , the company is getting more and more insights into its employees , and it hopes that in the long run , this will lead to a better bond of employees in a sector known for its high revenues .
&quot; If you are not obliged to create a culture of transparency , you will lose people , &quot; Vitrano said .
&quot; And if you lose people , you lose customers too .
And if you lose customers , you will lose companies .
The NCAA removes the two @-@ day practices and makes a long day for the chin .
It &apos;s time for 11 p.m. when a lot of UCLA football players are talking on one of the team &apos;s new practice fields on the campus .
Some of them have removed their trim parts and are showing their back full sweat from the first day of the training camp .
