Creation of a comic novel : A Castle in the History of England
In his recent past , the little Scotney Castle was shown in a squeeze music video and was a loophole for Margaret Thatcher in the 1970 &quot; s and 1980 &quot; s .
But Scotney has lived through 700 years of turbulent history from his cosy home in the Bewl River Valley in Kent and has now become the inspiration for a graphic novel by Jamie Rhodes , a mophaired Yorkshireman with a love for historical documents .
&quot; I think it &apos;s so nice to look at the handwriting of someone who has been dead for 300 years , &quot; he says wistfully .
&quot; What a personal relationship to keep this letter in my hands . &quot;
A Castle in England , spanning the Middle Ages to the Edward age , documents the families living in Scotney , and each story has been illustrated by another emerging British artist - Isaac Lenkiewicz , Briony May , William Exley , Becky Palmer and Isabel Greenberg .
It is not the first book by Rhodes based on an archive : he wrote in 2014 &apos; Dead Men &apos;s Teeth and Other Stories &apos; , a collection inspired by documents in the British Library - a project he found unexpectedly emotional .
&quot; I only responded to the form of the thumb on the side of a letter written by a double agent in the 1700 &quot; s who worked for the Jakobiner , &quot; he says .
&quot; This is his final print !
And letters always smell of smoke , because at that time a fire was ignited for the light .
For someone who lives 300 years later , the smell has an effect as if he were present himself .
Oh , I love archives ! &quot;
For someone beaten with old papers , supported by the National Trust and the Arts Council of England , and living in a castle for almost four months ( &quot; To be honest , I have a little overvalued my welcome greeting &quot; ) , this all seems too beautiful to be true .
Rhodos spent his days exploring the Victorian &quot; new &quot; castle , browsing the archives and studying the many treasures and curiosities of the castle , such as the assembled hyänenköps on the walls and the bottles that had been in the ditch for centuries .
He walked through the grounds and ruins of the old castle and talked with landscape writers , gardeners and sometimes artists working on the illustrations of his book .
Then , at night he drank &quot; Whiskey and began to write . &quot;
The intimate history of Scotney is relatively unknown , as the National Trust only got full access in 2006 , when the last heir , Elizabeth Hussey , died .
When Rhodes arrived a decade later , the employees just started to take care of the archive he described as &quot; hundreds of years when aristocrats said &quot; Oh , throw it on the attic . &quot;
Diary @-@ Books , Letters from the time of the English Empire , maps that show who owned what : &quot; It might be thought that land conflicts would be boring , &quot; Rhodes says , &quot; but there was a birch fought for by the two families .
Guys , let everything go its way ! &quot;
Some of the stories in the collection use the castle as a starting point for a broader immersion in English history , including &apos; The Labourer &apos; , which follows a man who leaves the newly built castle to join the 1381 farm uprising .
&quot; There is a record of a revolt that appeared in the castle in 1380 , in this book entitled &apos; Knightly Families of Kent and Sussex &apos; , &quot; says Rhodes .
&quot; The peasant uprising started in this area and I thought that an uprising was a good start .
Maybe the two are connected ? &quot;
Other stories are deeply rooted in the history of the castle - like &apos; The Priest &apos; , which tells the story of the Jesuit patron , which the Darrell family hid for seven years during the English Reformation .
Or &apos; The Smuggler &apos; , which depicts the 18th @-@ century smuggler Arthur Darrell , who staged his own funeral by filling a coffin with stones ( a discovery made only years later when his coffin was excavated ) .
The Darrells are Rhodes &apos;s favourite family of Scotney : &quot; The lords seemed to be quite a crook , getting into debts again and again and spending money on things they couldn &apos;t afford .
Other generations of Darrell men solved their debt crises by marrying rich old women - I imagine them as a bunch of good looking boys , with a talent to promote heirs . &quot;
After his final assignment in the castle , Rhodes is looking for other unique writing experience .
He has spent two weeks in the Finnish wilderness on a comic novel about Elämänmäki , the site of an early 20th century sanatorium , which has been restored by nature ever since .
But Scotney has a special place for him .
&quot; I was sad to go back , &quot; he says .
It was like : &quot; Oh , now I have to finish again with the damned life . &quot;
