Military reforms in Turkey - Erdgan &apos;s fear of the army
In Ankara , the Supreme Military Council ( YAS ) advises the President of the Council on retirement and promotions in the officer corps in the beginning of August each year .
So also this week .
At first glance a normal process .
But nothing is routine for the Turkish armed forces one year after the attempted coup .
The meeting was no longer held in the General Staff building , as it used to be , but in the Prime Minister &apos;s office .
The five Vice @-@ Premier ministers as well as the ministers for defence , home affairs , justice and foreign affairs have now also taken part in the discussions under the chairmanship of the head of the government .
The civilian population in the YAS were thus in the majority .
The government is drawing the line in the military .
Chief of Staff Hulusi Akar remains in office , but the commanders of the army , the air forces and the navy were dismissed .
Since the failed coup , head of state , Recep Taylor , Recep Taylor , has fired 169 out of the 326 generals and admirals .
Over 10,000 soldiers were removed from the army ranks - mostly because of alleged connections to the movement of the exiled preacher Fethullah Gülen , whom Erdgan sees as the leader of the attempted coup .
In Ankara , negotiations have been held on 486 persons , who are alleged to have been directly involved in the attempted coup , since this week .
