Red meat , dairy products and chocolate could help to relieve IBD ( inflammatory bowel disease ) .
Red meat and chocolate can sound like a nightmare for your digestive system .
But a new study claims the exact opposite : a diet rich in meat @-@ based proteins and dairy products could alleviate the symptoms of patients with inflammatory bowel diseases .
The incurable condition , which affects about 1.3 million in the USA and 300,000 in the UK , causes stomach cramps and blows , recurring or bloody diarrhoea , weight loss and extreme tiredness .
While the affected are usually advised to avoid meat and dairy products in order to alleviate their symptoms , researchers at Washington University found that the essential amino acid tryptophan from proteins helps to develop immune cells that promote a tolerant intestine .
The senior researcher , Dr. Marco Colonna , explained that immune cells in the intestines patrol to ensure that harmful microbes that hide in the meal do not penetrate into the body .
Cells capable of causing inflammation are balanced by cells that promote tolerance and protect the body without damaging sensitive tissue .
If the balance tends to be too close to inflammation , inflammatory bowel disease may result .
Experiments have shown that a type of tolerance @-@ promoting immune cell occurs in mice that carry a particular bacterium in their ingestures .
And the bacterium requires Tryptophan - one of the proteins &quot; building blocks - to trigger the appearance of the cells .
Professor Colonna said : &quot; We have established a link between a bacterial type - Lactobacillus reuteri - which is a normal part of the intestinal microbiome and the development of a population of cells that promote tolerance .
The more Tryptophan had mice in their diet , the more they had of these immunocells . &quot;
He suggested that the combination of L. reuteri and a tryptophan rich diet promote tolerant , less inflammatory intestines , if the same system also affects humans .
The postdotractorian Dr. Luisa Cervantes @-@ Barragan investigated a kind of immune cell that promotes tolerance when she found that a group of study messengers had such cells , but another group of the same tribe , which was housed separately , did not have them .
It assumed that the difference had to do with the mice &apos;s intestinal microbials - the community of bacteria , viruses and fungi that normally live in the stomach @-@ intestinal tract .
The mice &apos;s diets were DNA sequenced and six bacterial species found , which were present in mice with the immune cells , but without them were missing in mice .
Mouse was raised in a sterile environment , so that they lacked a intestinal microbiome and did not develop this type of immune cells , and they obtained the L. reuteri and the immune cells .
In order to understand how the bacteria had an effect on the immune system , the researchers had L. reuteri grow in a liquid and then transferred small amounts of the liquid - without the bacteria - to immature immune cells isolated from the mice .
The immune cells developed into tolerant cells .
When the active component was purged from the liquid , it was found to be a by @-@ product of tryptophan metabolism , known as indol @-@ 3 lactic acid .
When researchers doubled the amount of tryptophan in the mice lining , the number of such cells increased by about 50 % , but when tryptophants were halved , the number of cells fell by half .
Dr. Cervantes @-@ Barragan explained that people have the same tolerant cells as mice , and that most of us protect L. reuteri in our stomach @-@ intestinal tracts .
It is not known whether the human development of tryptophan by @-@ products from L. reuteri is as with mice , but defects in genes relating to tryptophan have been found in people with inflammatory bowel disease .
She said : &quot; The development of these cells is probably something we want to promote , as these cells control inflammation on the inner surface of the intestines .
High tryptophants in the presence of L. reuteri may potentially lead to an increase in this population . &quot;
